# Fingerprints of confirmed false positives in historical revisions of
# ansible/secrets.example.yml, which is a template of placeholder values.
#
# Each entry is pinned to an exact commit, file, rule and line, so it cannot
# mask a future finding. The current revision of that file carries inline
# `gitleaks:allow` comments instead; these two entries exist only because CI
# scans full history and the inline comments do not apply retroactively.
#
# Verified before suppressing:
#
#   vault_thread_network_key is byte-identical to 00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff,
#   the OpenThread default test key published in the upstream project. It is not a
#   credential for this network; the real Thread dataset has always been encrypted.
#
#   vault_pds_plc_rotation_key is a human-readable placeholder phrase. The comment
#   directly above it documents `openssl rand --hex 16` as the generator, whose real
#   output is 32 lowercase hex characters with no separators. The placeholder is
#   lowercase letters with underscores, so it cannot be the value it stands in for.

